Return Type & Return Statement
How return Works
When a return statement is executed in a function:

That function terminates immediately.
Control returns to the function call place.
The call is replaced with the returned value.

Return Type

In the function definition, before the function name, provide a data type — called the return type.
If no value is to be returned → use void (means empty).

cint test() {
    int a = 5;
    return a;    // returns 5
}

void main() {
    int a;
    a = test();
    printf("%d", a);
}
// O/P: 5

Return Statement Variations
c1) return 5;        // returns 5
2) return 3.10;     // returns 3 (if int return type, truncates)  // CE
3) return 'a';      // returns 97
4) return 10;
   return 20;       // only first return executes → 10 (second: unreachable)
5) return 10+20;    // returns 30  (warning: unreachable code)
6) return;          // O/P: GIV (garbage value)
7) return → GIV

Key Rule: When a function returns a value, collecting that value into a variable is always optional.


Nested Function Calls
When there are nested function calls, the order of execution is from inner to outer.
cint dept(int a) {
    return ++a;
}

int main() {
    int a = 15;
    dept(dept(dept(++a)));
    printf("%d", a);
    return 0;
}
// a = dept(dept(dept(16)))
// O/P: 19

printf() Return Value
printf() returns the number of characters printed (including spaces, symbols).
cif(printf("%."; sq))    // ASCII value of ';' is 59
if(!printf("Happy"))    // ! of non-zero = 0 → else branch
    printf("Happy");
else
    printf("People");
// O/P: HappyPeople

Void Function with Return
cvoid test() {
    return 5;   // CE: void function can't return value
}

void test() {
    return 5;
}
void main() {
    int a;
    test();         // not assigning → CE (undefined behavior)
    printf("%d", a);
}
// O/P: GIV
